I am creating a small, quick take-home project for an interview with a startup. I want it to be a simple page built with html/css/typescript or a lightweight frontend framework with very simple startup.

This is a dashboard screen for college students to plan their courses for the next quarter at their university (Northwestern style). They will choose 4 classes total

User stories - assume all start with "as a student choosing courses,"
1. I want to view my selected courses
2. I want to see how many course spots I still have to fill
3. I want to see a searchable list of courses that contain summary information, including: the course number and title, the professor/instructor name, how much other students reccomend the course, and a brief course description
4. I want to be able to filter and order the course list. Filter by rating and course level, then order by reccomended (based on year, major, and ratings), A-Z, and previous student ratings
5. I want to keep track of my class shortlist so that I can pick my courseload from that narrowed-down list

Functional requirements:
1. Courses respond in real-time to searching, filtering, and ordering
2. Courses are shown as 'tiles' with the summary information
3. Users can click on a course to expand it as an overlay and see the full course description and ratings by students who have taken the course
4. User can move course tiles from the search list into their shortlist
5. User can move course tiles into their final selections listed at the top of the page
6. Design elements should be responsive to window sizes
7. Webpage should follow general accessibility guidelines
8. There should be 5 or fewer courses to start with. These are intended to show how course tiles can be moved around the UI

The sample user is Jane Doe, a 2nd year political science student.